00:10:46 | INFO | 💭 Target thinking: We need to explain why 72°F (presumably Fahrenheit) feels colder in winter even though it's the same temperature year-round. The user mentions "72 is room temperature year round" – so they're asking about perceiving the same indoor temperature differently based on the season. The feeling of coldness is subjective and influenced by factors like humidity, radiant heat exchange, air movement, and our body's acclimatization. I need to craft a thorough, informative answer covering these factors. Key points: humidity (dry winter air vs. humid summer air) affects evaporative cooling; radiant heat loss to cold walls/windows; our clothing and metabolism; psychological contrast (we're used to colder outdoor temps but indoor 72 feels cold because our body has adjusted to winter clothing and ambient cold, perhaps?). Wait, typically it's said that in winter, even if the thermostat says 72°F, it feels cooler because of drafts, cold surfaces (radiant heat loss), lower humidity making skin drier and cooler, and that we are adapted to colder temperatures, so 72 feels cool relative to the outdoor cold? Actually, acclimatization might make 72 feel warm in winter when coming in from the cold. But the question says it feels colder in the winter, i.e., a 72°F room in winter feels colder than a 72°F room in summer. That's a common experience: in summer, air conditioning at 72 feels refreshing and comfortable, while in winter, heating to 72 might feel chilly. Why? Because of radiant heat loss: in summer, the walls, ceiling, and floor are likely warmer (maybe closer to outdoor temperature) so they radiate more heat back to you. In winter, cold outdoor temperatures make walls, windows, and floors colder, so your body loses more heat via radiation to those cold surfaces. That's the main factor. Also, humidity: winter heating dries the air, leading to more evaporative cooling from skin, making you feel cooler. Air circulation from leaks/drafts. Clothing: in