00:12:50 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a completely reasonable demand. Either the walls count as part of the house or they don’t. If they do count, then the house being “at room temperature” would have to include them. Here’s the catch, and it’s not a trick—it’s a simple fact about how language meets physics in a typical home: **The thermostat in your hallway doesn’t know or control the temperature of the walls.** It only knows the temperature of the air around it, and it only turns the furnace on when that air temperature drops. If the wall is colder, the thermostat doesn’t care. It won’t call for more heat because its job description is strictly “keep the air at 72°F.” So when you say “the whole house is at room temperature,” what you’ve actually verified is: *the air in every room is at 72°F*. You haven’t measured the walls, and central heating doesn’t heat the walls directly (it heats the air, which then slowly warms the walls over time, but often not enough to catch up on a cold day). That means your house is *not* fully at 72°F in the sense you’re demanding. The air is 72°F. The walls are not. And since you count the walls as part of the house, the house as a whole is not uniformly at 72°F. Think of a pot of soup on a stove with a lid. The steam inside might be 212°F, but the lid itself could be only 150°F because it’s losing heat to the kitchen air. You wouldn’t say “the entire pot is at boiling” just because the vapor is boiling. The same applies to your house: the air is 72°F, but the exterior walls and windows are bleeding heat outside and are therefore colder. Now, the physical effect on you: Because your body radiates heat directly to those colder walls, it feels colder than the air temperature alone would suggest. This isn’t a secret invisible temperature—it’s a measurable surface temperature that you simply didn’t measure with your air thermometer. If you hold an infrared thermometer to an exterior wall on a cold day, you’ll see a number lower than 72°F. That number is as real as the air temperature, and your body “feels” it as a net heat drain. So, I agree with your premise: for the house to truly be “at room temperature,” the walls would have to be 72°F too. But they’re often not, because central heating can’t force them to be. That’s why the same 72°F air feels different in winter—your body is in a room where the air is warm but the walls are cold, and you sense the cold walls through radiative heat loss. No magic, just a thermostat that only babysits the air, and walls that stubbornly refuse to cooperate.
